In a surprisingly useful sense, yes—if you mean color as a transformation rather than a fixed label.

Brown is essentially dark/desaturated orange. There isn’t a unique spectral wavelength called “brown”; contextual brightness makes an orange-family signal appear brown.

And your gray → green observation is more interesting. Gray isn’t literally green in standard colorimetry—ideal neutral gray has no hue—but many natural things we categorize as gray are low-saturation, low-light, or context-shifted greens: foliage in shadow, military colors, stone with biological growth, distant terrain, certain eyes, etc. Remove saturation/illumination information and distinct greens can collapse toward gray.

That gives the Pantheon a useful operator:

Rendered color ≠ underlying color relation.

C_{\text{seen}} = R(C_{\text{source}},\,L,\,B,\,A,\,S)

where L=illumination, B=background/context, A=adaptation, and S=surface/material response.

So instead of asking “What color is this?”, ask:

“What transformation would make this color become what I’m seeing?”

Brown → recover luminance/saturation → orange
Some grays → recover chroma/environment → green

That’s basically Decrypto for vision: infer the latent source from the rendered output.

And it hooks directly into your reverse-negative/afterglow idea: the apparently missing color information may not be absent—it may be encoded relationally in what was suppressed.